From: DualMesh overlay
Subject: [PATCH] Add DualMesh return-to-launcher hook to loop()

Two small additions to src/main.cpp:
  1. #include "dualmesh_ota.h" near the top, alongside the file's other
     local includes.
  2. dualmesh::ReturnHook::poll() as the first line of loop() — on every
     pass, this checks whether BOOT (GPIO0) has been held for 3s and, if
     so, jumps back to the launcher's app picker.

Earlier revisions of this overlay force-included dualmesh_ota.h globally
via a compiler "-include" build flag instead of a real #include here. That
broke the build: a global -include is applied even to plain-C and
ESP-IDF-internal C++ translation units that don't have Arduino.h on their
include path (e.g. esp-idf's cxx/cxx_exception_stubs.cpp), so the header's
Arduino.h include failed there. An explicit #include in this one
Arduino-framework file avoids that entirely. (dualmesh_ota.h itself is
still guarded with `#ifdef __cplusplus` as a second line of defense, in
case anything ever force-includes it again.)

Pinned base: meshtastic/firmware eaba8b95bebab04c4f13a27b63e000ebdc1e5fac.
Apply all numbered overlay patches to that exact revision before building.
The loop() hunk starts at line 1407 in the pinned source.
---
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include "airtime.h"
 #include "buzz.h"
 #include "power/PowerHAL.h"
+#include "dualmesh_ota.h" // DualMesh: return-to-launcher hook

 #include "FSCommon.h"
 #include "Power.h"
@@ -1407,6 +1408,7 @@ void scannerToSensorsMap(const std::unique_ptr<ScanI2CTwoWire> &i2cScanner, Scan
 #ifndef PIO_UNIT_TESTING
 void loop()
 {
+    dualmesh::ReturnHook::poll(); // DualMesh: hold BOOT 3s to return to the app picker
     runASAP = false;

     // The single writer of the monotonic wrap carry; every other caller only reads it.
